Subject: Add `--use-distinct-wchar-t`, mirroring `--use-distinct-char16-t`

`wchar_t` is a distinct type in C++, and bindgen erases it: `IntKind::WChar`
survives the IR intact, but codegen renders it through
`Layout::known_type_for_size`, so a `wchar_t` and the plain integer of the same
width become the same token. A consumer which has to name the C++ type again -
a bridge generator, say, whose C++ compiler checks the function's exact type -
has nothing left to name it with.

The erasure loses a second fact along with the first. `wchar_t`'s width *and*
its signedness are the target's business: 32 bits on most Unix targets and 16
on Windows, `int` on x86-64 Linux and Darwin, `unsigned int` under AAPCS. The
IR keeps neither. Width is recovered from the layout, and signedness is not
recovered at all - `IntKind::WChar::is_signed()` answers a hardcoded `false`,
with a standing `TODO(emilio)` above it saying there is no way to know. So
`wchar_t` never sign-extends where it should, and the type bindgen chooses is
unsigned on every target.

`--use-distinct-wchar-t` emits a fake type called `bindgen_cwchar_t` instead,
exactly as `--use-distinct-char16-t` emits `bindgen_cchar16_t`, and leaves the
representation to the caller. That is also the only place the target's answer
is available: the caller's substitute is *compiled for* the target, so it can
select the right width and signedness with a `cfg` rather than infer them.
Off by default, so no existing output changes.

--- a/codegen/helpers.rs
+++ b/codegen/helpers.rs
@@ -277,6 +277,13 @@
